Now let's understand the bug. In `fromISO`, when `throwOnInvalid = true`, `DateTime.fromISO(e)` where e is a duration string like "PT5H30M" throws `InvalidArgumentError: Invalid DateTime` instead of returning an invalid DateTime. So the code's flow of trying DateTime first and then Duration fails.

The fix: check whether the string looks like a duration before trying DateTime? Or catch the error? Let's look at how the actual luxon upstream fixed this. In upstream luxon, `Interval.fromISO` was rewritten:

```js
static fromISO(text, opts = {}) {
    const [s, e] = (text || "").split("/", 2);
    if (s && e) {
      let start, startIsValid;
      try {
        start = DateTime.fromISO(s, opts);
        startIsValid = start.isValid;
      } catch (e) {
        startIsValid = false;
      }

      let end, endIsValid;
      try {
        end = DateTime.fromISO(e, opts);
        endIsValid = end.isValid;
      } catch (e) {
        endIsValid = false;
      }

      if (startIsValid && endIsValid) {
        return Interval.fromDateTimes(start, end);
      }

      if (startIsValid) {
        const dur = Duration.fromISO(e, opts);
        if (dur.isValid) {
          return Interval.after(start, dur);
        }
      } else if (endIsValid) {
        const dur = Duration.fromISO(s, opts);
        if (dur.isValid) {
          return Interval.before(end, dur);
        }
      }
    }
    return Interval.invalid("unparsable", `the input "${text}" can't be parsed asISO 8601`);
  }
```

